-- Q1: medium / L2 — SEC prohibited non-audit services for issuers
(1, 'Independence', 'Grant & Howell LLP audits Redstone Inc., a publicly traded SEC registrant. The engagement partner proposes that the firm also provide bookkeeping services to Redstone for a subsidiary''s operations. Under SEC independence rules, this arrangement is:', '["Prohibited regardless of audit committee approval", "Permitted if the audit committee pre-approves the service", "Permitted if the bookkeeping fees are less than 5% of total audit fees", "Permitted if a separate team performs the bookkeeping"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Correct (A): SEC Regulation S-X, Rule 2-01(c)(4)(i) explicitly prohibits bookkeeping and related services for audit clients that are SEC registrants. This is a bright-line prohibition that cannot be overcome by audit committee approval or other safeguards. Wrong (B): Although audit committee pre-approval is required for permitted non-audit services, it cannot authorize services that are categorically prohibited. Wrong (C): The 5% de minimis exception applies only to non-audit services that are not otherwise prohibited, whereas bookkeeping is a bright-line prohibition. Wrong (D): Using a separate team does not overcome SEC categorical prohibitions, unlike safeguards that can mitigate threats for permitted services.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: hard / L4 — Management participation threat evaluation
(1, 'Independence', 'Walters CPA Group audits Oakmont Manufacturing. The client asks Walters to help select and implement a new ERP system, including configuring user access controls, setting default approval workflows, and training staff. The engagement partner argues the firm can provide the service because Oakmont''s IT director will sign off on all configurations. Evaluate this arrangement under the AICPA conceptual framework.', '["The arrangement is acceptable because the client designated an IT director to oversee the project", "The arrangement likely creates an unacceptable management participation threat because configuring access controls and approval workflows constitutes making management decisions", "The arrangement is prohibited only if Walters also audits the ERP system controls", "The arrangement is acceptable because ERP implementation is a permitted non-audit service under all circumstances"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): AICPA Code ET 1.295 prohibits assuming management responsibilities. Configuring access controls and approval workflows involves making decisions that directly affect the client''s operations and internal controls, creating a management participation threat. Client sign-off alone is insufficient when the CPA is making the underlying decisions. Wrong (A): Although the client designated an overseer, management oversight requires the client to make the substantive decisions rather than merely approve the CPA''s choices. Wrong (C): The management participation threat exists regardless of whether the CPA later audits those specific controls, unlike a self-review threat which requires auditing one''s own work. Wrong (D): ERP implementation services are not automatically permitted; they must be evaluated for management participation threats in contrast to routine advisory services.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Audit committee pre-approval and de minimis exception
(1, 'Independence', 'Fletcher & Drake LLP provides tax compliance services to Zenith Corp., a public company audit client. The tax engagement was initiated at the CFO''s request without audit committee involvement. The total tax fee is $12,000, representing 3% of the $400,000 total audit fee. The CFO plans to inform the audit committee at its next quarterly meeting. Under SEC rules, which safeguard must be applied?', '["No safeguard is needed because tax compliance is always a permitted service", "The audit committee must ratify the engagement retroactively before the next quarterly meeting", "The service must be promptly brought to the audit committee''s attention and approved before the audit is completed", "The firm must withdraw from the tax engagement immediately"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct (C): SEC Rule 2-01(c)(7) requires audit committee pre-approval of all non-audit services. The de minimis exception (under 5% of total fees) allows services to proceed if they are promptly brought to the committee''s attention and approved before the audit is completed. At 3%, this qualifies for the de minimis exception. Wrong (A): Even permitted non-audit services require audit committee pre-approval, unlike what this choice suggests. Wrong (B): The SEC rule requires approval before the audit is completed, rather than retroactive ratification by a specific deadline. Wrong (D): Withdrawal is not required if the de minimis exception conditions can still be met; however, the firm must act promptly to obtain approval.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: hard / L3 — SOX concurring review partner rotation and cooling-off
(1, 'Independence', 'Morris LLP audits Pacific Holdings, a public company. Partner Chen has served as the concurring review partner on the Pacific Holdings engagement for five consecutive years ending in 2025. Under SOX partner rotation rules, determine when Partner Chen may next serve in any capacity on the Pacific Holdings engagement.', '["2026, after a one-year cooling-off period", "2028, after a two-year cooling-off period", "2031, after a five-year cooling-off period", "2030, because the cooling-off begins immediately in the sixth year"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct (C): Under SOX Section 203 and PCAOB Rule 3600T, the concurring review partner must rotate after five consecutive years and is subject to a five-year cooling-off period. After serving through 2025, the five-year cooling-off runs 2026 through 2030, allowing return in 2031. Wrong (A): A one-year cooling-off applies to employment at the audit client under SEC rules, whereas partner rotation requires a longer cooling-off period. Wrong (B): A two-year cooling-off applies to other significant audit partners, not the concurring review partner who is subject to a more restrictive five-year period. Wrong (D): Although the cooling-off begins in 2026, the full five-year period extends through 2030 with return permitted in 2031.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — Independence in mind definition
(1, 'Independence', 'What does the term independence in mind mean under the AICPA Code?', '["The auditor''s actual state of objectivity, integrity, and professional skepticism", "The perception of independence by a reasonable third party", "The auditor''s compliance with all specific independence rules", "The auditor''s freedom from financial interests in any entity"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Correct (A): AICPA Code ET 0.300.040 defines independence in mind as the state of mind that permits the auditor to act with integrity, objectivity, and professional skepticism. It refers to the auditor''s actual, internal ability to remain unbiased. Wrong (B): This describes independence in appearance rather than independence in mind; appearance focuses on third-party perception. Wrong (C): Although compliance with rules is important, it does not define the concept of independence in mind, which concerns the auditor''s actual mindset. Wrong (D): Independence in mind is about the auditor''s mental state, not merely the absence of financial interests in contrast to specific financial interest rules.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Six core principles of AICPA Code
(1, 'Independence', 'Which of the following is one of the six core principles of the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct?', '["Confidentiality", "The public interest", "Transparency", "Efficiency"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): The AICPA Code of Professional Conduct establishes six core principles: Responsibilities, The Public Interest, Integrity, Objectivity and Independence, Due Care, and Scope and Nature of Services. The Public Interest principle requires CPAs to act in a way that serves the public trust. Wrong (A): Although confidentiality is addressed within the Code, it is a rule rather than one of the six core principles. Wrong (C): Transparency is not named as one of the six core principles, unlike The Public Interest which explicitly appears in the Code. Wrong (D): Efficiency is not one of the six core principles; however, Due Care encompasses competence and diligence.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: easy / L1 — AICPA Code Part 1 scope
(1, 'Independence', 'Part 1 of the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct applies to which group of CPAs?', '["Members in business working in industry", "Members in government agencies", "Members in public practice such as auditors and consultants", "Retired members only"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct (C): The AICPA Code is organized into three parts. Part 1 applies to members in public practice, including auditors, tax practitioners, and consultants who serve external clients. Part 2 applies to members in business, and Part 3 covers other members. Wrong (A): Members in business are covered by Part 2 rather than Part 1, which is reserved for public practice. Wrong (B): Government CPAs are not separately categorized; however, they fall under whichever part matches their role. Wrong (D): Retired members fall under Part 3 (Other Members), unlike active practitioners in public practice who are governed by Part 1.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q8: hard / L4 — Financial reporting oversight role (FROR) and cooling-off analysis
(1, 'Independence', 'Baxter & Lane LLP audits Orion Systems, a public company. Sarah Kim, a former senior manager on the Orion engagement, left the firm in January 2025 and immediately accepted a position as Orion''s Director of Internal Audit. She provided more than 10 hours of audit service during the most recent engagement. Evaluate whether Baxter & Lane''s independence is impaired.', '["Independence is not impaired because Kim was a manager, not a partner", "Independence is not impaired because the Director of Internal Audit reports to the audit committee rather than the CFO", "Independence is not impaired because Kim did not serve as lead or concurring partner", "Independence is impaired because Kim entered a financial reporting oversight role within the one-year cooling-off period"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct (D): SEC Rule 2-01(c)(2)(iii)(B) requires a one-year cooling-off period before any member of the engagement team who provided more than 10 hours of service can accept a financial reporting oversight role at the audit client. Director of Internal Audit is an FROR. Kim''s immediate transition violates this requirement. Wrong (A): The SEC cooling-off rule applies to any engagement team member who provided more than 10 hours, not only partners, unlike what this choice implies. Wrong (B): Although the Director of Internal Audit may report to the audit committee, the reporting line does not change the FROR classification because the role directly influences financial reporting oversight. Wrong (C): The 10-hour threshold applies regardless of whether the individual was a lead or concurring partner, in contrast to the rotation rules which distinguish between partner types.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q9: hard / L4 — De minimis exception threshold analysis
(1, 'Independence', 'Harper CPA Firm audits Vertex Inc., a public company, with total audit fees of $600,000. During the year, three non-audit services were performed at management''s request without prior audit committee approval: tax compliance ($18,000), transfer pricing study ($8,000), and compensation benchmarking ($5,000). The services were brought to the committee''s attention in aggregate. Does the firm meet the SEC de minimis exception?', '["Yes, because the total non-audit fees of $31,000 round down to meet the 5% threshold", "No, because the aggregate non-audit fees exceed 5% of total fees paid to the auditor", "Yes, because each individual service is under 5% of the total audit fee", "No, because the de minimis exception requires each service to be separately disclosed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): SEC Rule 2-01(c)(7)(i)(C) provides a de minimis exception when aggregate non-audit fees do not exceed 5% of total fees paid to the auditor. Here, $31,000 is approximately 5.2% of $600,000, exceeding the threshold. The exception is unavailable, and the lack of pre-approval impairs independence. Wrong (A): The SEC does not round down; the 5% threshold is a firm limit, and 5.2% exceeds it rather than qualifying under the exception. Wrong (C): The SEC evaluates the aggregate of all non-audit fees, not each service individually, unlike what this choice suggests. Wrong (D): Although prompt notification is required, the primary issue here is the aggregate fee exceeding 5%, which disqualifies the firm from the de minimis exception entirely.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — Non-audit services client oversight requirement
(1, 'Independence', 'Burke & Associates LLP provides payroll processing services to Millstone Corp., a non-issuer audit client. The firm enters payroll data, calculates withholdings, and submits tax deposits based on parameters Millstone''s controller provides each pay period. The controller reviews and approves each payroll run before processing. Under the AICPA Code, this arrangement:', '["Impairs independence because payroll processing is a prohibited service for all audit clients", "Does not impair independence because the client''s controller provides parameters and oversees the service", "Impairs independence because the firm has custody of client funds when submitting tax deposits", "Does not impair independence because payroll is always a permitted non-audit service"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): AICPA Code ET 1.295.040 permits non-audit services when the CPA does not assume management responsibilities, the client designates a competent individual to oversee the service, and the client accepts responsibility for results. Here, the controller provides parameters and approves each run, satisfying the oversight requirement. Wrong (A): Payroll processing is not categorically prohibited for non-issuer audit clients under the AICPA Code, unlike certain services prohibited for SEC registrants. Wrong (C): Submitting tax deposits based on the client''s approved calculations is an administrative function rather than custody of assets requiring management decisions. Wrong (D): Although payroll is not inherently prohibited, no non-audit service is automatically permitted; each must meet the management responsibility and oversight conditions.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Automatic license revocation
(1, 'Independence', 'Under what circumstance may a CPA''s professional license be automatically revoked without a hearing?', '["Failure to complete continuing education requirements", "Conviction of a felony or crime involving moral turpitude", "Receiving a peer review rating of deficient", "Failure to respond to a professional ethics inquiry within 30 days"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): Under most state accountancy laws and the AICPA bylaws, a CPA''s membership and license can be automatically revoked upon conviction of a felony or a crime involving moral turpitude, without the need for a separate disciplinary hearing. Wrong (A): Although failure to complete CPE may lead to license suspension, it typically requires notice and an opportunity to cure rather than automatic revocation. Wrong (C): A deficient peer review rating triggers corrective action; however, it does not result in automatic license revocation unlike a felony conviction. Wrong (D): Failure to respond to an ethics inquiry may lead to disciplinary proceedings but not automatic revocation, in contrast to the immediate effect of a criminal conviction.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Applying safeguards to familiarity threat
(1, 'Independence', 'Nelson & Price LLP identifies a familiarity threat on the Bridgeway Corp. audit because the engagement partner and the client''s CEO have been close personal friends for 20 years. The firm determines the threat is above an acceptable level but believes safeguards can address it. Which safeguard would be most effective?', '["Requiring the engagement partner to disclose the friendship in the engagement letter", "Obtaining audit committee pre-approval of the engagement partner''s continued service", "Increasing the sample size for substantive testing of revenue", "Assigning an independent partner not involved in the engagement to review all significant judgments"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct (D): AICPA Code ET 1.210.010 identifies engagement quality review by an independent partner as an effective safeguard against familiarity threats. An independent reviewer can objectively assess whether the engagement partner''s judgments were influenced by the personal relationship. Wrong (A): Disclosure acknowledges the threat but does not reduce it to an acceptable level, unlike an independent review which provides substantive oversight. Wrong (B): Although audit committee approval is a governance mechanism, it does not directly address the familiarity threat between the partner and management. Wrong (C): Increasing sample sizes addresses detection risk rather than the familiarity threat to objectivity, which requires personnel-level safeguards.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — SEC one-year cooling-off for lead partner employment
(1, 'Independence', 'Reeves & Clark LLP audits Summit Financial, a public company. The lead engagement partner, Taylor Ross, resigns from the firm in June 2025 and is offered the position of Chief Accounting Officer at Summit Financial. Under SEC independence rules, the earliest Ross may begin serving in this role without impairing the firm''s independence is:', '["June 2026, after a one-year cooling-off period", "June 2028, after completing the partner rotation cooling-off", "Immediately, provided the firm assigns a new lead partner", "June 2030, after a five-year cooling-off period"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Correct (A): SEC Rule 2-01(c)(2)(iii)(A) requires a one-year cooling-off period before the lead engagement partner can serve in a financial reporting oversight role at the audit client. Chief Accounting Officer is an FROR, so Ross must wait until at least June 2026. Wrong (B): The partner rotation cooling-off governs when the partner may return to the engagement at the firm rather than employment at the client. Wrong (C): Although assigning a new partner is necessary, it does not waive the SEC''s one-year cooling-off requirement for the departing partner''s employment. Wrong (D): A five-year cooling-off applies to partner rotation back to the engagement, not to employment at the client, unlike the one-year SEC employment restriction.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Self-review threat safeguard for financial statement preparation
(1, 'Independence', 'Grant & Parker LLP prepares the compiled financial statements for Beacon Industries, a non-issuer, and is also engaged to perform the audit. During the audit, the engagement team discovers a material misstatement in the compiled financials that the firm prepared. Applying the AICPA conceptual framework, which action best addresses the self-review threat?', '["Disclose the compilation service in the audit report", "Assign personnel not involved in the compilation to evaluate the misstatement and determine the appropriate adjustment", "Have the engagement team members who prepared the financials also correct the misstatement to ensure consistency", "Withdraw from the audit engagement because the self-review threat cannot be mitigated"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): AICPA Code ET 1.295 addresses the self-review threat. The most effective safeguard is to separate the personnel who prepared the financial statements from those evaluating the audit findings. Assigning independent team members to assess the misstatement reduces the risk that the preparers will be reluctant to identify errors in their own work. Wrong (A): Disclosure in the audit report does not reduce the underlying self-review threat, unlike personnel separation which provides substantive mitigation. Wrong (C): Having the same individuals correct their own work reinforces rather than mitigates the self-review threat. Wrong (D): Although withdrawal may be necessary in some cases, it is required only when no safeguard can reduce the threat to an acceptable level; personnel separation is an available safeguard here.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — Intimidation threat from threatened litigation
(1, 'Independence', 'During the audit of Coastal Manufacturing, the client''s legal counsel sends a letter to Adams CPA Group warning that any qualified audit opinion will trigger a $2 million breach-of-contract claim against the firm. Under the AICPA conceptual framework, this situation most directly represents which category of threat?', '["Advocacy threat", "Self-interest threat", "Intimidation threat", "Familiarity threat"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct (C): AICPA Code ET 1.210.010 defines intimidation as being deterred from acting objectively through actual or perceived threats, including threatened litigation. The legal counsel''s warning of a lawsuit designed to deter a qualified opinion is a textbook intimidation threat. Wrong (A): Advocacy involves promoting a client''s position rather than being threatened by the client, which is a distinct threat category. Wrong (B): Self-interest involves the auditor''s own financial interest influencing judgment, whereas intimidation involves external threats from the client. Wrong (D): Familiarity involves close relationships impairing objectivity, not threats of legal action, unlike intimidation which specifically addresses coercive pressure.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Other significant audit partners rotation requirements
(1, 'Independence', 'Singh & Patel LLP audits National Bancorp, a public company. Partner Diaz has served as the lead partner for the audit of National Bancorp''s largest subsidiary for seven consecutive years ending in 2025. Diaz is not the lead engagement partner or concurring review partner for the consolidated engagement. Under PCAOB partner rotation rules, determine the required cooling-off period before Diaz may return to the subsidiary engagement.', '["No cooling-off period is required for subsidiary audit partners", "A one-year cooling-off period before returning to the engagement", "A two-year cooling-off period before returning to the engagement", "A five-year cooling-off period before returning to the engagement"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct (C): Under SOX Section 203 and PCAOB rules, other significant audit partners (such as the lead partner on a major subsidiary) must rotate after seven consecutive years and observe a two-year cooling-off period before returning. This is a shorter cooling-off than the five years required for the lead engagement and concurring review partners. Wrong (A): Although Diaz is not the lead or concurring partner, significant subsidiary partners are subject to rotation requirements under PCAOB rules. Wrong (B): A one-year cooling-off applies to SEC employment restrictions rather than partner rotation, which has different timeframes. Wrong (D): A five-year cooling-off applies to the lead engagement partner and concurring review partner, whereas other significant partners require only a two-year cooling-off.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — State board vs AICPA disciplinary authority
(1, 'Independence', 'A CPA violates the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct by failing to maintain independence during a non-issuer audit engagement. Both the state board of accountancy and the AICPA investigate and determine the violation warrants discipline. Which entity has the authority to revoke the CPA''s license to practice?', '["Only the state board of accountancy, because it grants and controls the CPA license", "Only the AICPA, because the violation involved its Code of Professional Conduct", "Both the state board and the AICPA share equal authority to revoke the license", "The SEC, because all independence violations fall under federal jurisdiction"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Correct (A): State boards of accountancy are the sole authorities that grant and revoke CPA licenses. The AICPA can suspend or terminate membership, but it cannot revoke a CPA''s license. These are separate disciplinary tracks with different enforcement powers. Wrong (B): Although the violation involved the AICPA Code, the AICPA can only suspend or terminate its own membership rather than revoke a state-issued license. Wrong (C): The AICPA and state boards have different powers; however, only the state board can revoke the license. Wrong (D): The SEC has jurisdiction over auditors of public companies, whereas this violation involved a non-issuer outside SEC authority.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — PCAOB and SEC sanctions authority
(1, 'Independence', 'Which organization has the authority to impose sanctions on auditors for independence violations involving public companies?', '["The Federal Reserve Board", "The PCAOB and the SEC", "The Internal Revenue Service", "The Financial Accounting Standards Board"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): The PCAOB and the SEC both have authority to impose sanctions for independence violations involving public company audits. The PCAOB conducts inspections and disciplinary proceedings under SOX, while the SEC has broader enforcement authority over securities law violations, including auditor independence. Wrong (A): The Federal Reserve Board regulates banking institutions rather than auditor independence, which falls under SEC and PCAOB oversight. Wrong (C): Although the IRS administers tax law, it does not have jurisdiction over auditor independence standards. Wrong (D): FASB sets accounting standards; however, it has no enforcement or disciplinary authority over auditors, unlike the PCAOB.', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
